.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm libelfobj
library provides a C API for reading, creating, modifying, and writing
ELF (Executable and Linkable Format) object files.
It supports both 32-bit and 64-bit ELF formats, little-endian and big-endian
byte orders, and multiple architectures through a plugin-based relocation
backend system.
.Pp
The library operates on
.Vt elfobj_t
handles which represent parsed or constructed ELF objects.
An ELF object contains sections, symbols, and relocations that can be
queried and manipulated through the library's API.
.Ss Object Lifecycle
Objects are created in one of two ways:
.Bl -bullet
.It
By parsing an existing ELF file using
.Fn elf_open
or
.Fn elf_open_memory .
These objects are initially read-only.
.It
By constructing a new object using
.Fn elf_create .
These objects are mutable until finalized.
.El
.Pp
Once an object is no longer needed, it must be released using
.Fn elf_close ,
which frees all associated resources.
.Ss Functions
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn elf_open "path" "out"
Opens and parses the ELF file at
.Fa path .
On success,
.Fa *out
is set to a newly allocated
.Vt elfobj_t
handle.
The returned object is marked read-only; attempts to modify it will fail
with
.Dv ELF_ERR_STATE .
.It Fn elf_open_memory "buf" "size" "out"
Parses an ELF image from memory.
The buffer at
.Fa buf
must contain a valid ELF image of
.Fa size
bytes.
The library makes a private copy of the buffer.
On success,
.Fa *out
is set to a newly allocated handle.
.It Fn elf_create "type" "machine" "cls" "endian"
Creates a new empty ELF object.
.Fa type
is the ELF type
.Po Dv ET_REL ,
.Dv ET_EXEC ,
.Dv ET_DYN ,
or
.Dv ET_CORE
.Pc .
.Fa machine
is the architecture
.Po e.g.,
.Dv EM_386
.Pc .
.Fa cls
is either
.Dv ELFOBJ_CLASS_32
or
.Dv ELFOBJ_CLASS_64 .
.Fa endian
is either
.Dv ELFOBJ_ENDIAN_LE
or
.Dv ELFOBJ_ENDIAN_BE .
Returns NULL on allocation failure.
.It Fn elf_write_file "obj" "path"
Writes the ELF object to a file at
.Fa path .
The object must have been created with
.Fn elf_create
or modified after opening.
For objects opened with
.Fn elf_open ,
this function returns
.Dv ELF_ERR_STATE
unless the object has been explicitly made writable.
.It Fn elf_finalize "obj"
Prepares an object for writing by computing section layouts,
building string tables, and resolving internal references.
This is called automatically by
.Fn elf_write_file
if not already done.
Once finalized, the object becomes read-only.
.It Fn elf_close "obj"
Releases all resources associated with the ELF object.
After this call,
.Fa obj
is invalid and must not be used.
Passing NULL is safe and has no effect.
.It Fn elf_errstr "err"
Returns a human-readable string describing the error code
.Fa err .
The returned string is static and must not be freed.
.El
.Ss Error Codes
The
.Vt elf_err_t
enumeration defines the following error codes:
.Bl -tag -width "ELF_ERR_UNSUPPORTED"
.It Dv ELF_OK
Operation completed successfully.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_IO
Input/output error (file not found, read error, write error).
.It Dv ELF_ERR_FORMAT
Invalid or malformed ELF format.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_RELOC
Relocation processing error.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_OOM
Out of memory.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_BOUNDS
Offset or size exceeds file bounds.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_UNSUPPORTED
Feature not supported for this object.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_STATE
Invalid object state (e.g., modifying a read-only object).
.It Dv ELF_ERR_NOTFOUND
Requested entity not found.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn elf_open ,
.Fn elf_open_memory ,
.Fn elf_write_file ,
.Fn elf_finalize ,
and
.Fn elf_validate
return
.Dv ELF_OK
on success or an appropriate error code on failure.
.Pp
.Fn elf_create
returns a pointer to a new
.Vt elfobj_t
on success, or NULL on failure.
.Pp
.Fn elf_errstr
returns a pointer to a static string describing the error.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
The
.Nm libelfobj
library provides the following thread-safety guarantees:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Fn elf_errstr
is thread-safe.
.It
.Fn elf_register_reloc_backend
is thread-safe for concurrent registration of different architectures,
but concurrent registration of the same architecture is undefined.
.It
All other functions operating on the same
.Vt elfobj_t
handle are NOT thread-safe.
Callers must provide external synchronization when sharing objects
between threads.
.It
Different
.Vt elfobj_t
handles may be used concurrently without synchronization.
.El
.Sh MEMORY OWNERSHIP
.Bl -bullet
.It
Objects returned by
.Fn elf_open ,
.Fn elf_open_memory ,
and
.Fn elf_create
are owned by the caller and must be freed with
.Fn elf_close .
.It
Section data returned by
.Fn elf_section_data
is owned by the object and remains valid until
.Fn elf_close
is called or the section data is replaced.
.It
Symbol and section handles remain valid for the lifetime of the
containing object.
.It
Diagnostic strings returned via
.Fn elf_validate
must be freed by the caller using
.Xr free 3 .
.El
.Sh ABI STABILITY
The
.Nm libelfobj
library maintains a stable ABI.
Programs linked against
.Pa libelfobj.so.1
will continue to work with future releases of the library without
recompilation.
.Pp
The following are guaranteed to remain stable:
.Bl -bullet
.It
All public function signatures declared in
.In elfobj.h .
.It
Layout and values of the
.Vt elf_err_t
enumeration.
.It
Layout and values of the
.Vt elfobj_class_t
and
.Vt elfobj_endian_t
enumerations.
.It
The
.Vt elf_reloc_backend
structure layout.
.El
.Pp
The following are NOT part of the stable ABI:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Layout of
.Vt elfobj_t ,
.Vt elf_section_t ,
.Vt elf_symbol_t ,
and
.Vt elf_reloc_t
.Pq opaque types .
.It
Private functions and internal data structures.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Creating a Minimal ELF Object
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_section_t *text;
    unsigned char code[] = {0xC3};  /* ret instruction */

    /* Create a new 32-bit little-endian relocatable object for i386 */
    obj = elf_create(ET_REL, EM_386, ELFOBJ_CLASS_32, ELFOBJ_ENDIAN_LE);
    if (obj == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to create ELF object\\n");
        return 1;
    }

    /* Add an executable .text section */
    text = elf_add_section(obj, ".text", SHT_PROGBITS,
                           SHF_ALLOC | SHF_EXECINSTR);
    if (text == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to add section: %s\\n",
                elf_errstr(ELF_ERR_OOM));
        elf_close(obj);
        return 1;
    }

    /* Set section contents */
    if (elf_section_set_data(text, code, sizeof(code)) != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to set section data\\n");
        elf_close(obj);
        return 1;
    }

    /* Write to file */
    if (elf_write_file(obj, "minimal.o") != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to write ELF file\\n");
        elf_close(obj);
        return 1;
    }

    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;
}
.Ed
.Ss Reading an ELF Object
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_section_t *sec;
    elf_err_t err;
    size_t i;

    if (argc < 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <elf-file>\\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    err = elf_open(argv[1], &obj);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to open %s: %s\\n",
                argv[1], elf_errstr(err));
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Sections in %s:\\n", argv[1]);
    for (i = 0; i < elf_section_count(obj); i++) {
        /* Iterate sections by finding them; see elf_section.3 */
    }

    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;
}
.Ed
